      "message": "I\u0027ve been mulling over this for the last day, since this implements the approach that was outlined in b/280793619 pretty directly, and fixes the immediate problem around dst read flushes breaking clipping.\n\nBut since we also need to choose the renderer before the call to the new `applyClipToDraw` in order to fix b/273924867, since we need to know if the renderer uses coverage *before* we calculate the draw bounds that applyClipToDraw computes.\n\nI think the simpler approach at this point is to revert the changes to ClipStack, and update `Device::chooseRenderer` to not take a `Clip` object. Instead, it can take the `Geometry` and the `Transform`, and if we get down to the `preferWedges` calculation, we do a simple draw bounds calculation for the heuristic along the lines of:\n\n    Rect drawBounds \u003d transform.mapRect(shape.bounds());\n    if (!drawBounds.intersect(fClipStack-\u003econservativeBounds()) {\n        return nullptr;\n    }\n    // Use drawBounds\u0027s area in the heuristic instead of the old clip.bounds().area()\n    \nSince we only need this heuristic when it\u0027s a filled path using an MSAA renderer, the complexities of the final draw bounds calculations in `applyClipToDraw` fall away and should produce something very close to this. A single mapRect() and intersection that are possible redundant with the ClipStack\u0027s calculations are unlikely to show up in a profiler. And since it\u0027s just a visible pixel area heuristic for the renderer choice, any mismatch between these estimated bounds and the final bounds doesn\u0027t really matter.\n\nWDYT?",
